@startuml SAML_Federation_ADFS_STS
title SAML 2.0 Federation: On-prem AD + ADFS -> AWS STS -> DynamoDB\n(Example: alice@corp.example.com, AWS account 111122223333)

autonumber
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true

actor "Alice\n(Browser)" as User

box "Corporate Data Center (On-prem)" #EEF4FF
  participant "Custom App\n(orders.corp.example.com)" as App
  participant "ADFS\n(adfs.corp.example.com)\nSAML IdP / issuer" as ADFS
  database "Active Directory\n(Domain Controllers)" as AD
end box

box "AWS Account 111122223333" #FFF4E5
  participant "IAM SAML Provider\nsaml-provider/CorpADFS\n(stores ADFS metadata + public cert)" as IdP
  participant "AWS STS" as STS
  participant "IAM Role\nrole/DynamoReadRole" as Role
  database "DynamoDB\nOrders table" as DDB
end box

== One-time setup (done by admins, before any login) ==
ADFS -> IdP : Admin uploads ADFS FederationMetadata.xml into IAM\n(creates arn:aws:iam::111122223333:saml-provider/CorpADFS)
note over IdP
  This is AWS's stored record of the ADFS it trusts.
  It holds ADFS's public signing certificate.
  AWS never calls ADFS or AD at runtime.
end note
IdP -> Role : Role trust policy names the SAML provider as Principal
note over Role
  Trust policy (who may assume):
  {
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": { "Federated":
      "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:saml-provider/CorpADFS" },
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithSAML",
    "Condition": { "StringEquals": {
      "SAML:aud": "https://signin.aws.amazon.com/saml" } }
  }
  Permissions policy (what it can do):
    dynamodb:GetItem, dynamodb:Query on table/Orders
end note
ADFS -> ADFS : Admin configures AWS as a Relying Party Trust\n+ claim rules mapping AD group "AWS-Dynamo-Readers"\n-> SAML Role attribute

== Runtime: user login (authentication happens ON-PREM) ==
User -> App : Opens orders.corp.example.com
App --> User : Not logged in -> redirect to ADFS login page
User -> ADFS : GET ADFS sign-in page
ADFS --> User : Login page (served by ADFS, not AD)
User -> ADFS : Submit alice@corp.example.com + password
ADFS -> AD : Validate credentials (Kerberos/LDAP)
AD --> ADFS : Valid. Groups: AWS-Dynamo-Readers
note right of AD
  AD = source of truth for identity.
  This is the ONLY place the
  password is checked.
end note

ADFS -> ADFS : Build SAML assertion (XML) and\nSIGN it with ADFS private key
note over ADFS
  Assertion includes:
  - Subject / NameID: alice@corp.example.com
  - Attribute .../SAML/Attributes/RoleSessionName = alice
  - Attribute .../SAML/Attributes/Role =
      "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/DynamoReadRole,
       arn:aws:iam::111122223333:saml-provider/CorpADFS"
  - Audience, NotOnOrAfter (expiry)
  - Digital signature
end note
ADFS --> User : Signed SAML assertion (base64)
User -> App : POST SAML assertion to app

== Exchange assertion for temporary AWS credentials ==
App -> STS : sts:AssumeRoleWithSAML(\n  SAMLAssertion = <base64>,\n  RoleArn = ...:role/DynamoReadRole,\n  PrincipalArn = ...:saml-provider/CorpADFS )
note right of App
  This call is UNSIGNED:
  the app has no AWS keys yet.
  The signed assertion IS the proof.
end note

STS -> IdP : Look up the SAML provider named in PrincipalArn
IdP --> STS : ADFS public certificate
STS -> STS : Validate the ASSERTION (not the password):\n1. Signature valid with ADFS cert? (genuine, untampered)\n2. Not expired? Audience correct?\n3. Role + provider pair present in assertion?
STS -> Role : Check trust policy: does it allow\nFederated principal saml-provider/CorpADFS\nto call sts:AssumeRoleWithSAML?
Role --> STS : Allowed

STS --> App : Temporary security credentials:\nAccessKeyId (ASIA...), SecretAccessKey,\nSessionToken, Expiration (default 1 hour)

== Use the credentials ==
App -> DDB : Query Orders table\n(request signed with SigV4 using temp creds)
DDB -> Role : IAM evaluates role PERMISSIONS policy\n(assumed-role/DynamoReadRole/alice)
Role --> DDB : dynamodb:Query allowed
DDB --> App : Order items
App --> User : Render orders page

note over User, DDB
  Summary of who does what:
  - AD: authenticates the user (checks password)
  - ADFS: translates AD identity into a signed SAML assertion
  - IAM SAML Provider: AWS-side record of the trusted ADFS (public cert)
  - STS: validates the assertion signature + role trust policy, issues temp creds
  - IAM Role: trust policy = WHO can assume; permissions policy = WHAT they can do
end note

@enduml
